# Git

Canonical for this repo **and** for `mcp-execution-engine` / `mcp-gateway`. Those repos point here — do not fork a second copy of these habits.

## When

- Do **not** commit, push, or open a PR unless the human asks.
- Working-tree **inspection** (branch, dirty files) is fine. Do not stash, checkout, create, rename, or push branches unless asked.
- A **push** ask includes the [pre-push CI](#before-a-push) loop: fix failures and commit those fixes without a second ask, then push.

## Three repos

A “please commit”, “please commit and push”, “please commit, push, and open PRs”, or similar ask is **all three** checkouts (`website`, `mcp-execution-engine`, `mcp-gateway`) — not only the repo in focus. Commit (and push / open a PR, if asked) in every repo that has a change, or is ahead of the default branch. Skip a clean tree / nothing to PR. Do not open a second PR if one already exists for the branch.

## Where git runs

This Cursor window is a **multi-root** workspace. The default shell cwd is often `ethereumjs-monorepo`, not these three repos.

Every git command for FYP (inspect included: `status`, `diff`, `log`) must:

1. Set `working_directory` to **that** repo’s root — `…/feelyourprotocol/website`, `…/mcp-execution-engine`, or `…/mcp-gateway`. Never the monorepo, never `feelyourprotocol/` as a parent folder.
2. Use `required_permissions: ["all"]`. The sandbox cannot open the monorepo’s cursorignored `packages/ethereum-tests` submodule; `git status` there dies with `error opening 'packages/ethereum-tests/.git': Operation not permitted`.

If you see `packages/ethereum-tests` in a git error, you are **not** in a FYP repo. Do not `cd` into that submodule or “fix” it. Re-run with the two bullets above.

Inspect of the three siblings may run in parallel **only** when both bullets are set. Signed commit/push stay one-at-a-time (below).

## Signing

Commits **and** pushes are signed (SSH / Touch ID). The human confirms with a fingerprint; that can take a few seconds. Wait for the prompt.

Do **one** signed git operation at a time. Parallel commits or pushes fail — one fingerprint cannot answer three agents. After a commit-and-push ask: commit each dirty repo in turn, then push each repo that is ahead in turn.

Do not retry in a tight loop, skip signing, or abandon because the signer has not answered yet.

## Before a push

For **each repo that will be pushed**, run that repo’s local CI (the cheap gates GitHub runs). Do this **before** the push, after the work is committed or about to be.

| Repo | Local CI |
| --- | --- |
| `website` | `npm run lf:ci` · `npm run type-check` · `npm run test:unit:ci` |
| `mcp-execution-engine` | `npm run typecheck` · `npm run test:ci` · `npm run lf:ci` · `npm run build` |
| `mcp-gateway` | `npm run typecheck` · `npm run test:ci` · `npm run lf:ci` · `npm run build` |

Skip Cypress / `test:e2e` unless the change is Cypress or a user-visible flow that job already covers.

If a gate fails: fix it, re-run until green, **commit** the fix (same [commit-message](#commit-messages) habits) without waiting for another “please commit.” Then push. Do not push red.

Same `working_directory` + `required_permissions: ["all"]` as other git. CI is not a signed git op — it may run in parallel across the three repos.

## After a push

A push does not say whether a PR exists. For **each repo you just pushed**, look it up (`gh pr view <branch>`).

- **PR exists:** update **that** PR’s body so it still matches the [arch shape](#pull-requests) and the work now on the branch. Refresh **Round-trip** (done vs open) and **So far**. Do not retitle a round-trip PR. Do not turn the body into a changelog of the last commit. Do not open a second PR.
- **No PR:** say so. Open one only if the human asked to open PRs.

Report the PR URL (or “no PR”) after the push. `gh pr edit` is not a signed git op — run it after the push, still with `working_directory` + `required_permissions: ["all"]`.

## Commit messages

One line. Name the artifact **and** what changed — a bare `EIP-8038 MCP` is too thin. No conventional-commit prefix (`feat:`, `fix:`), no body, no invented PR numbers (`#52` is added on merge).

Normally **7–10 words**. Compact, not a paragraph.

```
EIP-7843 SLOTNUM exploration and cover
EIP-8038 MCP module and run_bytecode message-call
EIP-8038 Bro & Bruh comic
Twitter reply voice refinement
```

- EIP work: `EIP-NNNN` + artifact + a short change clue (what this commit actually landed).
- Otherwise Title Case at the same length (`Launch Strategy draft`, `CI job budget`).
- If unsure, read `git log --oneline` on that repo and add one more specific noun than a generic artifact label.

## Pull requests

Same shortness as commit subjects. No conventional-commit prefix. No template checklist.

**Round-trip** (`eip-NNNN` branch, or the session is a protocol-change round-trip): every repo’s PR is the **bigger-context** PR. Title is just the EIP id (`EIP-8038`) — including engine/gateway, even if the first commits are only supporting (git rules, scaffolding). That PR will accumulate the exploration / MCP work. Do not name it after the first commit.

Description: the **arch**, not the latest commit. Same shape, **not** the same text — each repo writes its own slice.

- **website** — the exploration (widget, cover, home, mcp-docs twin).
- **mcp-execution-engine** — the engine module / verbs / catalog.
- **mcp-gateway** — transport: exposing those verbs, no per-EIP tool.

Within that slice:

- **What** — one or two lines: this repo’s part of the EIP.
- **Round-trip** — where this repo sits (exploration vs MCP vs later); mark done vs open.
- **So far** — what shipped **here**; what still lands on this PR. After later pushes, this is the line that moves.

Do **not** copy the website body onto engine/gateway. Do **not** narrate supporting commits (`git.mdc`, templates). Those are not the PR.

Engine and gateway bodies **link** the website GitHub PR (the exploration). Do not paste its description.

- After that main PR: follow-ups add the artifact (`EIP-8038 Comic + Video`) and a short artifact-specific body.
- Outside a round-trip: two-to-five-word title (`Git rules`); short structured body is fine.
